            <ArticleTitle>Discal hernia in children and teenagers: medical, surgical and recovery treatment.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>477-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Lumbar disc hernia represents a rare situation for the physician. The first intervention in disc hernia was performed during the '40. The rate of surgery needing lumbar hernia is about 1-2%. Lumbar disc hernia in children and teenagers has 4 main causes: familial history, trauma, congenital malformation of the spine and disc degeneration. The symptoms in young patients are dominated by local or ischiadic irradiated pain, but neurological discrepancies rarely occur.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
